<h1>Bounds benchmark</h1>

<p>
    This page exists to answer one question, so that the remaining Phase 4 items in
    <code>ai-plans/active/performance-improvements.md</code> are decided by a measurement instead of
    by a guess: <strong>does anything build a bounds over enough points for it to matter?</strong>
</p>

<div class="BenchNote">
    <p><strong>What is being measured, and why it is measured this way.</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            The timings below are of <strong>library operations</strong> &mdash;
            <code>dataLayer.getBounds()</code> and <code>map.addToBounds()</code> &mdash; not of
            <code>bounds.extend()</code> in a tight loop. A loop over two million points would produce an
            impressive number and "prove" the optimization is worth doing whether or not any real code
            path does that. Only the operations a real application actually calls can decide this.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>getBounds()</code> is timed rather than <code>fitBounds()</code> because
            <code>getBounds()</code> is the bounds walk on its own. <code>fitBounds()</code> also moves the
            Google viewport, which would swamp the thing being measured.
        </li>
        <li>
            The optimizations do not exist yet, so there is nothing to compare against. Instead each
            scenario also runs a <strong>numeric floor</strong>: the same coordinates reduced with plain
            <code>Math.min</code>/<code>Math.max</code> over a <code>Float64Array</code>, allocating
            nothing. The gap between the two is the <strong>ceiling</strong> on what the optimization
            could recover.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>That ceiling is not reachable.</strong> For the data layer, Google's own
            <code>forEachLatLng()</code> hands back a <code>google.maps.LatLng</code> per coordinate no
            matter what this library does. D-3 can drop the <code>latLngConvert()</code> wrapper and the
            literal that <code>extend()</code> stores, but not Google's object. Treat the gap as an upper
            bound that the real change will fall short of.
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>

<h2>Scenario A &mdash; data layer bounds (D-3, and C-5 underneath it)</h2>

<p>
    <code>DataLayer.#bounds()</code> walks every coordinate of every feature and calls
    <code>latLngConvert()</code> then <code>extend()</code> on each one. It runs on every
    <code>getBounds()</code> and <code>fitBounds()</code>, and on any <code>addGeoJson()</code> or
    <code>loadGeoJson()</code> with <code>fitBounds: true</code>. This is the largest real caller in
    the library &mdash; a detailed boundary or trail GeoJson is tens of thousands of coordinates.
</p>

<p>
    Set the feature count and points per feature to match your real data. For a trail site, that is
    roughly one feature per trail and however many points your tracks carry.
</p>

