<h2>How to read the verdict</h2>

<p>
    The verdict applies one rule, and the rule is about the <em>saving</em>, not the total. An operation
    that takes 400&nbsp;ms is irrelevant if the change can only recover 3&nbsp;ms of it.
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Under 5&nbsp;ms of ceiling</strong> &mdash; not worth doing. Leave the item struck.</li>
    <li><strong>5 to 50&nbsp;ms</strong> &mdash; worth doing only if it is also a simplification. Judge it on the code, not the clock.</li>
    <li><strong>Over 50&nbsp;ms</strong> &mdash; worth doing. This is a real cost a user can feel on a page load.</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Run this on the slowest device you support, not on the Mac. The iPhone is the reason this plan
    exists, and a number gathered on a desktop browser does not decide anything for it. Connect the
    phone to the Mac and use Safari's Web Inspector.
</p>

